  "worked_solution": "Recall that the hyperbola ${x}^{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=1$ for $x\\ge 1$ in $xz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n\u27e8x,z\u27e9=\u27e8\\operatorname{sec}\u2061t,\\,4\\operatorname{tan}\u2061t\u27e9,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =(\\operatorname{sec}\u2061t)^{2}-\\frac{(4\\operatorname{tan}\u2061t)^{2}}{16} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}\u2061t-\\frac{16{tan}^{2}\u2061t}{16} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}\u2061t-{tan}^{2}\u2061t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,y) & =\u27e8x,y,z\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\operatorname{sec}\u2061t,y,\\,4\\operatorname{tan}\u2061t\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $\\operatorname{sec}\u2061t.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
